<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span id="Ax1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_align_center">Appendix of VLA-Adapter</span></h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Setup Details of LIBERO Simulation Benchmarks</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LIBERO benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> comprises four distinct task suites: LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, and LIBERO-100. The first three suites each contain 10 tasks, and LIBERO-100 contains 90 short-term tasks (LIBERO-90) and 10 long-horizon tasks (LIBERO-Long). The strategy for each task depends solely on the current instructions provided. Each task is repeated multiple times (50 repetitions in this paper) to obtain the average success rate for each subtask. The examples and the instructions in the LIBERO benchmark are shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure A1 ‣ Appendix A Setup Details of LIBERO Simulation Benchmarks ‣ VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09372v1/Appendix-LIBERO.png" id="A1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:543/273;" width="543" height="273" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure A1: </span>The examples and the task instructions on the LIBERO benchmark.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the LIBERO benchmark, we use third-person images (resolution 224<math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>224<math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3, RGB) and wrist images (resolution 224<math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>224<math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3, RGB) as visual input. The task instruction is first constructed as a prompt in a specific format: “<span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">In: What action should the robot take to instruction.lower()?\nOut:</span>”, and then input into the VLM module together with the image information. Its output is a 7-dimensional action vector, which is used to control the 7-DOF Franka Emika Panda simulated robot arm to perform the corresponding action sequence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>DiT-Based Policy Network</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Overall Architecture</h3>

<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09372v1/DiT_policy.png" id="A2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:247/316;" width="247" height="316" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B1: </span>The DiT-based policy network.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This architecture is shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure B1 ‣ B.1 Overall Architecture ‣ Appendix B DiT-Based Policy Network ‣ VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B1</span></a>. It consists of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-DiT blocks, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\tau\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\tau\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. It has the same number of layers as VLM. Each DiT block consists of three components: conditional modulation, conditional attention, and a conditional feedforward network.
At timestep <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the input chunk to the first-DiT block, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a noisy action sequence. Since the input contains random noise, to facilitate the transition from “pure noise <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> fine-grained prediction”, we adopt the AdaLN-Zero layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles &amp; Xie, 2023</a>)</cite> to modulate the activation amplitude at each layer. The AdaLN-Zero consists of LayerNorm, modulation, and gated residual. Specifically, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mathcal{M}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mathcal{M}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> will be obtained by <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mathcal{M}}_{t}=\sigma^{\prime}_{1}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}})+\sigma_{0}(\mathcal{P}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mathcal{M}}_{t}=\sigma^{\prime}_{1}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}})+\sigma_{0}(\mathcal{P}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is used to generate “Scale” and “Shift” vectors, which guide the activation direction of intermediate features and inject automatic modulation amplitude via gated residual control. After modulation, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf A}^{1}_{t}=\left[\widetilde{\bf a}_{t}^{1},\widetilde{\bf a}_{t+1}^{1},\dots,\widetilde{\bf a}_{t+H-1}^{1}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf A}^{1}_{t}=\left[\widetilde{\bf a}_{t}^{1},\widetilde{\bf a}_{t+1}^{1},\dots,\widetilde{\bf a}_{t+H-1}^{1}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf A}_{t}^{1}={\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}+{\alpha_{\tau}}\odot\varepsilon({\gamma_{\tau}}{\mathop{\rm LN}\nolimits}({\bf{A}}_{t}^{1})+{\beta_{\tau}})={\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}+{\sigma^{\prime}_{2}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})\odot\varepsilon({\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}^{(2)}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}){\mathop{\rm LN}\nolimits}({\bf{A}}_{t}^{1})+{\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}^{(1)}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})),\ [{\beta_{\tau}};{\gamma_{\tau}}]={\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">LN</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">LN</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf A}_{t}^{1}={\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}+{\alpha_{\tau}}\odot\varepsilon({\gamma_{\tau}}{\mathop{\rm LN}\nolimits}({\bf{A}}_{t}^{1})+{\beta_{\tau}})={\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}+{\sigma^{\prime}_{2}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})\odot\varepsilon({\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}^{(2)}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}){\mathop{\rm LN}\nolimits}({\bf{A}}_{t}^{1})+{\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}^{(1)}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})),\ [{\beta_{\tau}};{\gamma_{\tau}}]={\sigma^{\prime}_{1}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B-1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\beta_{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\beta_{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\gamma_{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\gamma_{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> are scaling factors and offset factors, which are dynamically generated by <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> through a projection new with the SiLU. <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha_{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha_{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gated residual coefficient, used to adjust the injection amplitude. It is dynamically generated by <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sigma_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sigma_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the SiLU, and has <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha_{\tau}}={\sigma^{\prime}_{3}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha_{\tau}}={\sigma^{\prime}_{3}}(\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> is element-wise multiplication, and <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is self-attention and projection modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After conditional modulation, <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf{A}}_{t}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is used as the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QKV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QKV</annotation></semantics></math> vector, and <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}},\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{AQ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{R}},\mathcal{C}_{t}^{\mathcal{AQ}}</annotation></semantics></math> are used as the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KV</annotation></semantics></math> vectors for Bridge Attention. The details of Bridge Attention are shown in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Policy with Bridge Attention ‣ 3 VLA-Adapter Methodology ‣ VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. And then, the attention latent <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bf{A}}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bf{A}}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> will be obtained. <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bf{A}}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bf{A}}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is input into the conditional feedforward network. The first-DiT block output <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}^{2}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}^{2}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained. After passing through <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> DiT blocks, we get the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bf A}^{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bf A}^{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is passed by a LayerNorm and MLP layer to generate the current action chunk <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf A}^{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf A}^{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Training of DiT-Based Policy</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This Policy is also trained from scratch. Given a ground truth action trajectory <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bf{A}}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bf{A}}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a noisy action in DiT-based Policy <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{A}}_{t}^{\tau}=\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}{{\bf{A}}_{\rm{t}}}+\sqrt{1-{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{A}}_{t}^{\tau}=\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}{{\bf{A}}_{\rm{t}}}+\sqrt{1-{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative product of noise coefficients, and has <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}=\prod\nolimits_{i=1}^{T}{{\alpha_{i}}}=\prod\nolimits_{i=1}^{T}{(1-{\beta_{i}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub></msqrt><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{{{\overline{\alpha}}_{\tau}}}=\prod\nolimits_{i=1}^{T}{{\alpha_{i}}}=\prod\nolimits_{i=1}^{T}{(1-{\beta_{i}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variances used at each step, and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\bf{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,{\bf{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gaussian noise. We train DiT-based model <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with the training objectives:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
